---
# What CrowdSec reads on this box THAT IT COULD NOT WORK OUT FOR ITSELF.
# Written by bootstrap.sh — edit here, in git, and re-run it.
#
# THIS FILE IS DELIBERATELY ALMOST EMPTY, AND THAT IS THE POINT.
#
# The CrowdSec installer runs `cscli setup`, which detects the services on the
# box and writes its own acquisition into the same directory:
#
#   setup.sshd.yaml     /var/log/auth.log            type: syslog
#   setup.caddy.yaml    /var/log/caddy/*.log         type: caddy
#   setup.haproxy.yaml  /var/log/haproxy.log         type: haproxy
#   setup.linux.yaml    /var/log/syslog, kern.log    type: syslog
#
# That covers every public lane on this box already. An earlier version of this
# file listed all four again, which did not fail — it duplicated them. The
# generated files carry a warning about exactly that ("make sure the log sources
# are not acquired twice"), and the agent log showed the consequence plainly:
#
#   Starting tail ... file=/var/log/caddy/access.log
#   Starting tail ... file=/var/log/caddy/access.log     <- twice
#
# Double acquisition is not a crash. Every line is read, parsed and poured into
# a bucket twice, so every scenario reaches its threshold at half the intended
# rate — a config that bans people twice as eagerly as it claims to, with
# nothing anywhere reporting it. Prefer the generated files; add only what they
# miss.
#
# HOW TO TELL WHAT THEY MISS: `cscli metrics` lists one row per acquired source,
# and the agent log names every file it tails at startup. If a source appears
# there already, it does not belong in this file.

# squid — the egress proxy. `cscli setup` has no squid detection, so this is the
# one source it will never add on its own.
#
# Reachable only over the tailnet (or from the house, if PUBLIC_FALLBACK=true),
# so this is not an attack surface in the way the public lanes are. It is
# acquired for a different reason: this log is where a STOLEN PROXY CREDENTIAL
# would show up, as authenticated requests from somewhere that is not the
# cluster. Nothing bans on it today — there is no stock scenario for "this proxy
# user appeared from a new address" — but the lines have to be being read before
# any such scenario could ever fire.
source: file
filenames:
  - /var/log/squid/access.log
labels:
  type: syslog
